To test it: one, stores opened in each of the two years. Two, revenue per mature store, both years. Three, contribution margin per mature store. Four, contribution margin per new store. Five, average months to breakeven. Six, pre-opening cost per store. Seven, D&A by year. Eight, rent dollars by year.

If mature-store margin is flat and the decline is concentrated in new stores, it's an expansion problem. If mature stores also compressed, it's a cost-input problem and I'd go back to COGS.
